  "id": "two-person-psychology",
  "term": "two-person psychology",
  "definition": "Two-person psychology is a psychoanalytic perspective that understands therapy as shaped not only by the patient and analyst separately, but also by their shared dyadic process. In this view, meaning and new ways of relating emerge through the interaction between them, sometimes described as an analytic third or unlocalized shared subjectivity.",
